when you put together this technological  revolution and a government mentality  that says we will do anything it takes  to stop terrorism what you have is  dangerous hi I'm Paul teacher caprice on  TV today we're talking with Adam  Schwartz of the ECL you in Illinois  we're going to talk about fusion centers  and stalking us out of pleasure to be  talking to you for those out there that  don't know what fusion centers are could  you just give us a brief description  sure in the wake of the 911 terror  attacks the US government has spent  billions of dollars to have the US  Department of Homeland Security Fund  state and local fusion centers these are  state organizations that fuse under one  roof as much information as possible  about people who are suspected of  criminal or terrorist and then they  funnel this information towards the  Department of Homeland Security and what  kind of information are we talking about  here well if a person is of interest to  the Illinois fusion center the Illinois  fusion center has access to a vast  number of databases about that person  including criminal databases run by  every level of government including  non-criminal government databases such  as head shots in the Secretary of  State's database the Illinois fusion  center also has access to private  databases and so they know about  everybody's business licenses their  employment history the identities of  their neighbors and relatives what  property they own what is the ACL u--'s  perspective on this what's the danger of  these fusion centers the first concern  is that a person might become the object  of fusion center scrutiny for no good  reason so for example the missouri  fusion center published report saying  that the followers of ron paul and the  people who display flags and say don't  tread on me might be involved in  domestic terrorism and not to be  scrutinized by american law enforcement  or for example the Virginia fusion  center published report concluding that  historically black colleges and  universities were nodes of radicalism  that should be under law enforcement  scrutiny in Maryland the future  center with scrutinizing opponents of  the death penalty law enforcement is  taking in all of this information  doesn't that lead to stopping crime  there is a recently published report by  the United States Senate bipartisan  report which examined what the US  government has been getting for its  money billions of dollars that has  channeled through the Department of  Homeland Security to the 70 or so state  and local fusion centers in America and  what this report concludes based on  reviewing hundreds and hundreds of the  reports coming from the fusion centers  to the Homeland Security Department is  that these reports are doing nothing to  protect us from terrorism that the  reports contain old information or  already published information or  information unrelated to terrorism and  perhaps of greatest concern to the sou  information that would violate the  Federal Privacy Act prohibition on the  federal government's collecting  information about people's First  Amendment activities in the absence of  suspicion of a crime is it possible for  viewers to look up the information that  a fusion center might have about them  there are safeguards that we think every  fusion sensor should have and one of  them is that people should be able to  find out whether or not the fusion  center has a file on them and if so to  get the file and to be able to petition  the fusion center to remove information  that they think is not correct and if  the fusion center refuses to do so to be  able to put their own statement into the  file that explains what they think is  wrong so that you know later on when  someone else in the law enforcement  community gets the file they can see the  person's rebuttal we are in the midst of  a technological revolution which  empowers the government in a way that  never previously existed to track what  innocent Americans are doing and to use  that information against us and in the  wake of the 911 terror attacks the  expectation for many people as the  government should use as many of these  new technologies as possible to do  everything possible to keep us as safe  as possible and when you put these two  things together all of our privacy and  all of our civil liberties is managed is  there any way of going back or is this  here to stay you know I think the  technological revolution obviously  cannot be prevented and it should not be  prevented by government from the view of  the ACLU there are many pro civil  liberties aspects of the technological  revolution but when you put together  this  technological revolution on the one hand  and a government mentality that says we  will do anything it takes to stop  terrorism or the possibility of  terrorism what you have is dangerous and  so really the first question ought to be  whenever the government proposes one of  these new technologies will the use of  this technology actually make us safer
 

